# Who reviews what (CIS Software Supply Chain Benchmark v1.0 §1.1.6/§1.1.7).
#
# MyKronos read `codeowners_coverage` as `unknown` for this repository, which it
# renders as "without one, a code-owner review requirement routes to nobody"
# (MyKronos B-060, TheHub story #60579). Same shape and wording as mykronos's
# .github/CODEOWNERS, so the estate reads one way.
#
# **This is a single-operator estate, and this file says so plainly rather
# than inventing teams.** Every line resolves to the same person, which makes
# the coverage number 100% and makes it mean exactly one thing: nothing here
# is owned by nobody. It does not mean review is distributed, and a file that
# implied otherwise would be worse than no file.
#
# `codeowner_review_required` is deliberately still off. Turning it on with
# these lines would deadlock every pull request — GitHub refuses to count a
# self-approval, which is the same trap MyKronos D-110 hit and recorded. This
# file is the routing; enabling the requirement is a decision for the day
# there is a second reviewer.
#
# Last match wins, so the catch-all goes first and the specific paths follow.

